******The Data Plane (Worker Nodes)**********
The Data Plane is responsible for actually executing the applications. Every worker node contains three essential components:
Kubelet: This is the primary "agent" on the node. It is responsible for creating and maintaining Pods, ensuring they are always in a running state
. If a Pod fails, the cubelet informs the control plane to trigger auto-healing
.
Container Runtime: This is the software responsible for actually running the container (e.g., containerd, CRI-O, or Docker shim)
. Kubernetes supports any runtime that implements the standard container interface
.
Kube Proxy: This component handles networking, including assigning IP addresses and managing load balancing
. It typically uses IP tables on the Linux host to route traffic between Pods and ensure that if a service is scaled, requests are distributed correctly
.
***The Control Plane (Master Node)*******
The Control Plane acts as the "brain" or heart of the cluster, managing instructions and maintaining the desired state
. Its components include:
API Server: The core component that exposes the Kubernetes API to the external world
. It receives all incoming requests and serves as the gateway for interacting with the cluster
.
Scheduler: When the API server receives a request to create a resource, the scheduler decides which worker node is best suited to host that resource based on availability
.
etcd: A key-value store that acts as the backing store for all cluster information
. It keeps a record of every object and configuration in the cluster, making it essential for backup and restoration
.
Controller Manager: This manages various built-in controllers, such as the ReplicaSet
. Its job is to ensure that the actual state of the cluster matches the desired state (e.g., if you request two Pods, the controller manager ensures two are running)
.
Cloud Controller Manager (CCM): An optional open-source utility used when running Kubernetes on cloud platforms like AWS, Azure, or GCP
. It translates Kubernetes requests into the specific API calls required by the cloud provider to manage resources like load balancers or storage

